<volume>130</volume><issue>33</issue><fpage>10836</fpage><lpage>10837</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">18652463</pub-id></mixed-citation></ref><ref id="b36-ijn-4-123"><label>36.</label><mixed-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Shiohara</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Hoshino</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Hanaki</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><name><surname>Suzuki</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><name><surname>Yamamoto</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>On the cytotoxicity caused by quantum dots</article-title><source>Microbiol Immunol</source><year>2004</year><volume>48</volume><issue>9</issue><fpage>669</fpage><lpage>675</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15383704</pub-id></mixed-citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-group><fig id="f1-ijn-4-123" position="float"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>Side view of two animals (<bold>A</bold> and <bold>B</bold>) injected with Alexa-labeled micelles, at one hour (left) and two hours (right) after the injection. Arrows point to the tumor.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ijn-4-123f1"/></fig><fig id="f2-ijn-4-123" position="float"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>Ventral view of the animal injected with Alexa-labeled micelles (<bold>C</bold>) and another animal injected with Kodak X-Sight 761 (<bold>D</bold>), at one hour (left) and two hours (right) after the injection.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ijn-4-123f2"/></fig><fig id="f3-ijn-4-123" position="float"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p>Cryosections of tumor, kidney, and liver removed from the animals injected with Alexa rhodamine-labeled micelles. Upper row: fluorescence microscopy imaging of rhodamine accumulation. Lower row: white field images of eosin and hematoxylin-stained sections.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ijn-4-123f3"/></fig><fig id="f4-ijn-4-123" position="float"><label>Figure 4</label><caption><p>Ventral view of an animal injected with Alexa-labeled micelles at the end of the experiment with the skin removed. Tumor is visible in the side of the animal.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ijn-4-123f4"/></fig><fig id="f5-ijn-4-123" position="float"><label>Figure 5</label><caption><p>Fluorescence intensity of the background signal around the tumor and the intrinsic fluorescence of the animal body (control region of interest [ROI] over hip). The overall signal fluctuated with time as the biodistribution of the contrast agent changed.</p><p><bold>Notes:</bold> N = 6; bars represent standard error of the mean.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ijn-4-123f5"/></fig><fig id="f6-ijn-4-123" position="float"><label>Figure 6</label><caption><p>Normalized signal-to-noise ratio for the fluorescence of the tumor. Data show the ratio of the tumor fluorescence versus the background in the area around the tumor.</p><p><bold>Notes:</bold> N = 6; bars represent standard error of the mean.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ijn-4-123f6"/></fig></floats-group></article>